name: searching text
boss: Murmur
flavor: I decide what your commands get to read.
brief: grep prints the lines that match a pattern. It is the single most
brief: useful command on this system, and most of its power is in six flags:
brief: -i ignore case, -v invert, -n number, -c count, -l name files, -r walk
brief: a tree.
brief:
brief: Patterns are regular expressions unless you say otherwise. -F turns
brief: that off and matches the text literally, which is what you want the
brief: moment your pattern contains a dot or a bracket you meant as itself.
---
q: {%show% every line of @log@ containing @sev@|which lines of @log@ mention @sev@?|pull the @sev@ lines out of @log@}
a: grep @sev@ @log@
out: @sev@
ref: grep
teach: grep
---
q: {%show% the @sev@ lines in @log@, ignoring case|find @sev@ in @log@ whether it is upper or lower case|match @sev@ in @log@ case-insensitively}
a: grep -i @sev@ @log@
out: @sev@
ref: grep
teach: grep -i
---
q: {%show% every line of @log@ that does NOT contain @sev@|which lines of @log@ are missing @sev@?|drop the @sev@ lines from @log@ and show the rest}
a: grep -v @sev@ @log@
out: .
ref: grep
teach: grep -v
hint: v for invert
---
q: {%count% how many lines of @log@ contain @sev@|how many @log@ lines mention @sev@?|report the number of @sev@ lines in @log@}
a: grep -c @sev@ @log@
out: ^[0-9]+$
cmp: ref
ref: grep
teach: grep -c
hint: this counts LINES, not occurrences -- two matches on one line count once
---
q: {%show% the @sev@ lines of @log@ with their line numbers|number the @sev@ matches in @log@|which line numbers in @log@ have @sev@?}
a: grep -n @sev@ @log@
out: ^[0-9]+:
cmp: ref
ref: grep
teach: grep -n
---
q: {search every file under here for @term@, recursively|%find% @term@ anywhere in the tree below you|grep the whole directory tree for @term@}
a: grep -r @term@ .
out: .
ref: grep
teach: grep -r
---
q: %list% only the NAMES of files under here that contain @term@
a: grep -rl @term@ .
out: .
ref: grep
teach: grep -l
---
q: %list% the files under here that do NOT contain @term@
a: grep -rL @term@ .
out: .
ref: grep
teach: grep -L
---
q: %show% only the matched text from @log@, not the whole line
a: grep -o @sev@ @log@
out: ^@sev@$
ref: grep
teach: grep -o
---
q: %show% lines of @log@ where @sev@ appears as a whole word, not inside another
a: grep -w @sev@ @log@
out: @sev@
ref: grep
teach: grep -w
---
q: %show% lines of @log@ that consist of exactly @sev@ and nothing else
a: grep -x @sev@ @log@
ref: grep
teach: grep -x
---
q: %find% @sev@ in @log@ and show the 2 lines after each match
a: grep -A 2 @sev@ @log@
out: @sev@
ref: grep
teach: grep -A
---
q: %find% @sev@ in @log@ and show the 2 lines before each match
a: grep -B 2 @sev@ @log@
out: @sev@
ref: grep
teach: grep -B
---
q: %find% @sev@ in @log@ and show 2 lines of context on either side
a: grep -C 2 @sev@ @log@
out: @sev@
ref: grep
teach: grep -C
---
q: %show% only the first 2 matching lines of @log@ and stop
a: grep -m 2 @sev@ @log@
out: @sev@
ref: grep
teach: grep -m
---
q: search @log@ for the literal text 0.0.0.0 -- the dots must not be wildcards
a: grep -F 0.0.0.0 @log@
out: 0\.0\.0\.0
ref: grep
teach: grep -F
hint: F for fixed string
---
q: %find% lines in @log@ holding an IP-like number, using extended regex
a: grep -E '[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+' @log@
out: [0-9]
cmp: ref
ref: grep
teach: grep -E
---
q: match either @sev@ or @term@ in @log@ in one extended-regex pass
a: grep -E '@sev@|@term@' @log@
out: .
ref: grep
teach: grep -E
---
q: check quietly whether @log@ mentions @sev@ -- no output, just an exit status
a: grep -q @sev@ @log@
ref: grep
teach: grep -q
hint: this is the form you use inside an if
---
q: search @log@ for @sev@ and suppress any error about unreadable files
a: grep -s @sev@ @log@
out: @sev@
ref: grep
teach: grep -s
---
q: search @log@ using each line of @txt@ as a separate pattern
a: grep -f @txt@ @log@
ref: grep
teach: grep -f
---
q: search @log@ for @sev@ naming the pattern explicitly with a flag
a: grep -e @sev@ @log@
out: @sev@
ref: grep
teach: grep -e
hint: -e is how you search for something starting with a dash
